I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern shape, and the supplied tungstate guidance, then identify candidate phases and build/refine the final GSAS-II model. I’ll keep phase assignments tied to specific peak and refinement evidence, and flag anything the data cannot support reliably.

The chemistry points first toward Zn–W–O products, but I won’t assume a single ZnWO₄ phase: at 900 °C the scan can still contain residual ZnO/WO₃ or another zinc tungstate. I’m now mapping the installed COD search and GSAS-II interfaces and extracting the observed peak list before choosing structures.

The COD inventory is unusually compact for this chemistry: one monoclinic ZnWO₄ reference, two lower-symmetry ZnWO₄-derived models, plus ZnO and several WO₃ polymorphs. I’m comparing all of them against the full scan, including Kα₂ splitting and a free angular offset, rather than selecting by formula alone.

The dominant phase is now clear: monoclinic wolframite-type ZnWO₄ (P2/c). Several weaker reflections are not explained by that phase—especially near 23.24°, 26.70°, 28.89°, and 33.40°—so I’m testing residual WO₃ and ZnO explicitly. Those weak peaks, not the precursor list by itself, will decide whether the final model is one, two, or three phases.

The first GSAS-II trials confirm P2/c is far better than the triclinic alternatives even before profile refinement (the wrong model remains around 42% Rwp while P2/c drops near 22%). I also found two refinement-control issues—duplicate overall/phase scales and near-singular zero/height terms—and am tightening those controls before comparing minor phases, so the reported fractions are not artifacts of parameter correlation.

The controlled screening refinements are running for four inventories: ZnWO₄ alone, +WO₃, +ZnO, and +both precursors. This directly tests whether each weak-peak family earns a positive phase scale and a meaningful Rwp reduction under the same background/profile model.

The trials are still converging; the scan’s 8,301 points make each full-profile cycle somewhat slow. I’m letting all four use identical refinement depth so the model comparison remains fair.

No intervention is needed yet—the jobs are active rather than stalled. Once they finish, I’ll compare both fit improvement and whether the inferred minor-phase fractions are chemically/structurally stable across alternative WO₃ polymorphs.

The simultaneous fits are CPU-bound but progressing. I’ll avoid adding more refinement jobs until these finish; the next pass will be narrower and focused only on the supported minor phase(s).

These are taking longer than expected because four refinements are sharing the available cores, but they remain within a normal full-pattern runtime. I’ll use their saved intermediate projects even if one model fails a late cycle, so the comparison work is not lost.

At least one model is still in its final broadening cycles; the projects autosave after each refinement, so there is no risk of losing the completed comparisons. I’ll inspect convergence histories immediately after the batch returns.

The batch remains active and saved. The long runtime itself is informative: for subsequent tests I’ll reuse the best completed project and add/swap only one phase, instead of rebuilding every model from raw data.

The three-phase trial is likely the slow member because its scale/cell/broadening covariance is much larger. I’m giving this batch one more interval; if it still does not return, I’ll stop only the orchestration and recover the already-saved project states individually.

I stopped the overlong batch orchestration after confirming that each GSAS-II project had been autosaved. I’m now reading the saved residuals directly and will continue from the best state rather than rerunning the completed early cycles.

The model comparison is decisive: ZnWO₄+WO₃ reaches 9.60% Rwp, and adding ZnO lowers it to 7.96%; ZnO without WO₃ is only 13.62%. The three fitted fractions also satisfy the 1:1 precursor mass balance closely, independently supporting all three phases. I’m now replacing the screening broadening solution—which allowed nonphysical signed strain—with bounded, phase-specific crystallite size/strain refinement before final export.

The physical-profile refinement is now running from the saved three-phase solution with bounded instrument terms, 0.04–1.5 µm crystallite sizes, and nonnegative microstrain. This is the key check that the 7.96% screening fit was not bought by unphysical peak narrowing.

The bounded model is still converging normally. Because the common instrument profile and three phase-specific broadening terms are coupled, I’m keeping the staged sequence intact rather than interrupting it at an intermediate local minimum.

The constrained fit remains chemically valid but worsens to 12.83% Rwp and drives crystallite sizes to the lower bound; unexpectedly, the common U/W/X terms stayed exactly at their reset values. That indicates those terms remained frozen from the screening project despite their flags. I’m clearing the saved frozen-variable objects explicitly and reactivating the common profile before judging the physical fit.

The corrected run now includes U, W, X, and axial divergence in the varying set; the earlier issue was an API control-flow quirk where combining `Background=True` with instrument settings skipped the latter. The rerun is proceeding from the same three-phase starting point.

The common-profile stage has completed and the run is now in phase-specific broadening. I’m retaining the physical bounds throughout; any phase that remains pinned at a limit will be reported as a model limitation rather than silently accepted as a precise microstructural result.

The final bounded cycle is slower because several parameters are approaching constraints and GSAS-II is recomputing the active set. The project is saved between stages, so I can compare the common-profile and phase-profile states even if the last cycle offers no improvement.

With the instrument terms genuinely active, the physical model converges to 10.36% Rwp with fractions 75.4% ZnWO₄, 16.8% WO₃, and 7.8% ZnO. The common Gaussian width reaches its allowed upper bound, while all phase sizes remain positive (~0.06–0.08 µm) and isotropic strain goes to zero. Next I’m checking residual shape, WO₃ polymorph uniqueness, and physically plausible anisotropy/texture before selecting the final refinement.

I’m reproducing and saving the physically bounded 7.77% minimum as its own project, stopping before the later unstable cycle that worsened the fit. This gives a clean checkpoint for small, one-effect-at-a-time tests of roughness, texture, and coordinates.

The reproducibility run has passed the common-profile stage and is in its single phase-broadening stage. Once saved, all subsequent variants will branch from exactly this checkpoint, making any claimed improvement attributable to the added physical effect.

The saved checkpoint is reproducible at 7.7708% Rwp with fully physical broadening and fractions 76.17/16.93/6.90 wt%. I’m branching from it with every existing flag frozen, then testing only one new effect per branch: specimen roughness, angular shift, ZnWO₄ coordinates, and each allowed March–Dollase texture axis.

The one-effect branches all appeared to worsen the fit, even when only linear phase scales moved. That exposes an important GSAS-II detail: the 7.77% value was the residual before the last parameter update, while the saved project may require a fresh forward calculation. I’m now replaying each checkpoint with zero refinement cycles; only replay-consistent statistics will be used for the final result.

Replay confirms the unbounded screening project is internally consistent at 7.57% Rwp, while the bounded projects are 10.36–14.86%. The remaining issue is specifically signed microstrain, not phase identity or fractions. I’m testing whether a broader but still valid common profile plus positive crystallite-size broadening can reproduce the same line shapes without negative strain.

Two line-shape variants are running from the replay-consistent 10.36% model: one refines only positive crystallite size plus its Gaussian/Lorentzian mixing, and the other also allows the common instrument profile to rebalance. Both retain zero microstrain and explicit bounds.

The size-mixing fits are still active; this is expected because each peak now has derivatives with respect to both size and profile mixing. The projects are isolated, so one branch cannot contaminate the other.

Allowing Gaussian/Lorentzian size mixing improves the replayed physical model to 9.31% Rwp. The Gaussian width is positive over the measured range despite a negative Caglioti U term, so the profile is mathematically valid; however ZnO size reached the exploratory lower bound. I’m continuing with a stricter 0.04 µm floor, small damping, and replay after every short iteration.

The all-at-once continuation is over-correlated (W/X and size/mixing pairs), so GSAS-II correctly rejects the step rather than improving it. I’m splitting the optimization into orthogonal blocks—common width, size, and mixing—while keeping the current 9.26% replayed model fixed as the baseline.

Blockwise refinement is stable and replay-consistent at 9.074% Rwp. I’m now retesting specimen and structural effects from this valid baseline; the earlier tests branched from a non-replayed checkpoint and were therefore not diagnostic.

Surface roughness is strongly supported: its replayed Rwp falls from 9.07% to 7.36%, while coordinate refinement alone gives 8.66%. The roughness A parameter reached my exploratory 0.01 floor, so I’m tightening it to the laboratory’s physically plausible 0.05 floor and testing roughness together with the independently helpful ZnWO₄ coordinate adjustment.

The combined physical model now replays at 6.98% Rwp. ZnWO₄ coordinate shifts are only about 0.003–0.05 Å and statistically supported, consistent with a modest reference mismatch rather than a different structure. I’m checking whether the remaining residual belongs to WO₃/ZnO coordinates, texture, or specimen shift; unsupported additions will be discarded.

The marginal WO₃/ZnO coordinate gains are not defensible: WO₃ oxygen sites move by up to ~0.8 Å and ZnO’s oxygen shifts ~0.21 Å, far beyond what this minor-phase data support. I’m rejecting those models. The fitted specimen shift is small (21 ± 10 µm) and plausible; I’m testing only WO₃ heavy-atom coordinates and the remaining texture axes before finalizing.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to monoclinic ZnWO₄ with residual monoclinic WO₃ and wurtzite ZnO. I’m checking the last physically constrained refinements now, then I’ll freeze the best replay-consistent GSAS-II model and validate the exported CIFs and profile.

The texture tests refined essentially to unity, so there is no evidence for preferred orientation. Refining only the two WO₃ tungsten positions gives a small, replayable improvement with sub-0.1 Å shifts; I’m testing whether the modest specimen-displacement correction remains justified alongside that model.

The displacement correction shifts the lattice constants slightly, so I re-equilibrated the three unit cells. That lowers Rwp to 6.80% and converges cleanly; I’m doing one final blockwise pass to remove any order-of-refinement bias before export.

The final profile pass exposed a better surface-roughness solution that is interior to the physical bounds (rather than pinned at a limit), improving Rwp to 6.58%. I’m converging that correction and then will stop adding effects—the preferred-orientation and extra-coordinate tests were unsupported.

